.\" __fpurge.3 - stdio_ext.h stream queries and buffer purge
.TH __FPURGE 3 "2026-10-21" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
__fpurge, __freading, __fwriting, __fpending \- inspect and purge a stream's buffer
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio_ext.h>
.PP
.BI "void __fpurge(FILE *" stream ");"
.BI "int __freading(FILE *" stream ");"
.BI "int __fwriting(FILE *" stream ");"
.BI "size_t __fpending(FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR __fpurge ()
discards everything
.I stream
has buffered: output not yet written, input read ahead but not yet
consumed, and a character pushed back with
.BR ungetc (3).
Nothing is written and the underlying file descriptor's offset is not
moved; contrast
.BR fflush (3),
which writes pending output and gives read-ahead back to a seekable file.
.PP
.BR __freading ()
returns non-zero if
.I stream
is open for reading only, or if its last operation was a read.
.PP
.BR __fwriting ()
returns non-zero if
.I stream
is open for writing only, or if its last operation was a write.
.PP
An update stream that has not yet been read or written, or has just been
flushed or repositioned, is neither reading nor writing.
.PP
.BR __fpending ()
returns the number of bytes of output
.I stream
holds that have not yet been written to the file.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR __fpurge ()
returns no value.
.BR __freading ()
and
.BR __fwriting ()
return non-zero or zero as described above.
.BR __fpending ()
returns a byte count, zero for a stream that holds no output.
.PP
None of these functions sets
.IR errno .
.SH NOTES
These functions are extensions provided under the same names by glibc and
Solaris.
gnulib looks for them before falling back to code that reads the fields
of
.I FILE
for a list of known C libraries, and stops the build on any other with
.RI \(dq "Please port gnulib fpurge.c to your platform!" \(dq
and similar.
.PP
.BR __fbufsize (),
.BR __flbf (),
.BR __freadahead ()
and the rest of the family are not provided.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following program buffers some output, then thinks better of it.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdio_ext.h>

int
main(void)
{
    fputs("never shown", stdout);
    printf("%zu bytes pending\en", __fpending(stdout));
    __fpurge(stdout);       /* both lines are dropped */
    puts("only this line is written");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fflush (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR __fseterr (3),
.BR setvbuf (3),
.BR ungetc (3)
.SH STANDARDS
None; these are nonstandard extensions.
